> do morji fi ny no'u la .anabeLIS.
> .i ji'a ny jmive pensi lo po'o si'o
> ny prami gi'e se prami vau mi
>

[Sorry if this is posted twice]

It should be pointed out that
1) 'many years' is translated { lo nanca be li so'i }. { so'i nanca } means
'many [not necessarily continuous] year-long periods.
2) { noi la'a cu'i do morji fi ny } is grammatical but odd. The usual
formulation would be { fi ke'a }, {ke'a} being the placeholder for the
antecedent in a relative clause.

Not saying there may not be poetic reasons for those choices, but I thought
it should be clear.

ui do ru'i cilre fi la lojban .i mu'o mi'e komfo,amonan

